# Instructions for AI agents

Copy this template to `AGENTS.md` in the repository root before using it.
`AGENTS.md` is ignored by Git; customize that local copy for your tools and environment.
This example is not automatically loaded as an instruction file.

## Project

Yggvion is a personal calendar, task planner, Markdown notebook and time tracker.
It works locally and optionally synchronizes encrypted data through a self-hosted Go server.

| Path | Responsibility |
| --- | --- |
| `client/lib/data/` | SQLite storage, repositories, synchronization and integrations |
| `client/lib/domain/` | Models and domain logic |
| `client/lib/ui/` | Flutter screens and widgets |
| `client/test/` | Client unit, widget and integration tests |
| `server/` | Go API, storage and server tests |
| `api/openapi.yaml` | Client/server contract |
| `deploy/`, `tool/`, `tools/` | Deployment, build helpers and checks |
| `docs/` | Features, architecture, setup and recovery documentation |

Read [CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md), [Architecture](docs/architecture.md)
and the documentation relevant to the task before changing behavior.

## Working rules

- Check `git status` and preserve existing uncommitted changes.
- Inspect the actual implementation and relevant tests before editing. Use available semantic navigation tools where helpful.
- Keep changes focused and follow existing patterns. Do not add dependencies or architecture layers without a concrete need.
- Do not commit, push, publish, deploy or perform destructive operations without explicit authorization.
- Do not edit generated files manually; update their sources and use the existing generator when applicable.
- Use synthetic data in fixtures, screenshots and examples. Never commit or log passwords, tokens, private keys or personal data.
- Preserve API compatibility unless a breaking change is explicitly requested. Update client, server, contract and tests together when the API changes.
- Report checks actually performed, unverified behavior and migration/compatibility implications. Do not describe skipped tests as passing.

## Flutter and Dart

- Preserve the existing Riverpod/repository separation. Keep network requests, SQL and business logic out of widgets.
- Use explicit types, null safety and predictable asynchronous error handling. Check `mounted` before using a context after an asynchronous operation.
- Dispose controllers/subscriptions and avoid expensive work during `build()`.
- Follow existing themes, localization, routing and responsive layouts. Consider loading, empty and error states.
- Test meaningful logic changes and regressions with the existing unit/widget/integration infrastructure.
- Use simulators/emulators or isolated profiles for automated checks. Do not install diagnostic builds on a physical device without explicit authorization.

## Go and the API

- Follow the existing packages, HTTP routing, configuration and database libraries.
- Propagate contexts and deadlines. Handle errors explicitly, preserve cancellation and check concurrent access for races.
- Validate authorization and input at the server boundary; use parameterized SQL, bounded request sizes and appropriate timeouts.
- Preserve TLS and SSH host verification. Do not expose private API routes through the public sharing listener.
- Respect the encryption boundary: clients validate decrypted personal content; the server validates access, encrypted envelopes, versions and quotas.
- Use transactions for related changes and the existing migration system for schema updates. Never erase real data as a shortcut.
- Add relevant handler/contract tests for API changes and use the race detector for concurrent code.

## Verification

Format changed Dart files with `dart format` and Go files with `gofmt -w`.
Run checks appropriate to the affected components:

```sh
# From client/
flutter analyze
flutter test

# From server/
go test ./...
go vet ./...
```

Run `flutter pub get` for initial setup or dependency changes. Use `go test -race ./...`
for concurrency changes. Do not run `go mod tidy` as unrelated cleanup.

See [Manual checks](CONTRIBUTING.md#manual-checks) for building the isolated Go test server,
setting `YGGVION_TEST_SERVER`, and running installer/public-page checks. Without that environment
variable, server-dependent client integration tests are skipped. CI is intentionally absent.

Before finishing, review `git diff` and run `git diff --check`. State any checks that could not run.
Do not access a real VPS, external backup account or existing user database for tests without authorization.
